                <h2>Case Opening: Three Ad Accounts Restricted Overnight</h2>
<p>At Monday's routine meeting, the acquisition team of a tool-based app noticed something odd: spend on the main Meta ad account had dropped from a daily average of $800 to under $200, and two freshly created backup accounts could not pass review at all. Twelve new videos had gone live in the previous week; five of them used absolute claims such as "100% eliminates" and "the only officially authorized", and the landing pages carried third-party logos without authorization.</p>
<p>The problem looked like "a rejected creative", but the real investigation uncovered three overlapping layers:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Surface level</strong>: new creatives triggered rules against exaggerated claims and misleading statements.</li>
<li><strong>Middle layer</strong>: the backup accounts shared the same payment method and device fingerprint as the restricted account, so they were pulled into a linked review.</li>
<li><strong>Root level</strong>: the team had never pre-screened creatives for compliance, and there was no risk isolation between top-ups and account limits — once the main account was restricted, the backups had no ammunition to run with.</li>
</ol>
<p>Simply replacing creatives and relaunching would only repeat the failure. This time, the team ran the full inspection SOP.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Determine Whether It Is a Creative Issue or an Account Issue</h2>
<p>When a restriction notice arrives, do not immediately rewrite creatives and relaunch. Spend 15 minutes on three things:</p>
<ul>
<li>Read the notification verbatim: distinguish "Ad rejected" from "Account restricted". The former is a single creative problem; the latter freezes the entire account's ability to run, top up and edit.</li>
<li>Identify the rejected creative IDs: export review records for the last 7, 14 and 30 days to see whether rejections cluster around one pattern.</li>
<li>Categorize the rejection reasons: exaggerated claims / misleading statements / personal attributes / brand and trademarks / invalid landing page. Clear categorization tells you what to fix.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Tip: build a mapping table of "rejection reason — creative type — compliant rewrite". The team then self-checks against it instead of guessing, which is far more efficient.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 2: Creative and Landing-Page Self-Check List</h2>
<p>The team pulled all 12 new creatives and screened them one by one. The five rejections clustered into three high-risk categories:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Risk item</th>
<th>Example from this case</th>
<th>Compliant rewrite</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Absolute wording</td>
<td>"100% eliminates", "best", "No.1"</td>
<td>Use quantifiable but non-absolute phrasing, e.g. "cuts the workflow to XX% fewer steps on average"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Unverified comparisons</td>
<td>"10x faster than competitors"</td>
<td>Remove the comparison, or add test conditions, timeframe and a verifiable source</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Brand and trademarks</td>
<td>Third-party logos and "officially authorized" on the landing page</td>
<td>Use own-brand visuals only; keep authorization documents on file</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Two other creatives were clean, but their landing pages embedded misleading countdown timers and forced pop-up redirects, which were flagged as "invalid landing page".</p>
<p>A good inspection list should settle into six fixed items: no absolute wording in claims, evidence behind every performance promise, traceable comparisons, authorized brand assets, landing page consistent with the ad message, and no deceptive pop-ups or redirects.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Account Tiering and Top-Up Risk Controls</h2>
<p>Once the creative issues were resolved, account-side isolation became the key to avoiding a total shutdown. The team made three adjustments:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Account tiering</strong>: separate accounts into "main / test / backup", each with its own admin email, payment method and operating environment. Never share devices or login credentials across accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Top-up isolation</strong>: the main account keeps 3–5 days of spend as buffer, test accounts are topped up in small amounts, and backup accounts serve emergency use only rather than routine campaigns.</li>
<li><strong>Balance alerts</strong>: trigger alerts when spend reaches 60% and 80% of account balance; block auto top-ups when balance falls below the threshold while the account is under review observation.</li>
</ul>
<p>The third point matters most here. The team had originally enabled "auto top-up when balance is low". After the main account was restricted, the automatic charge failed and triggered platform risk control, further delaying recovery. Turning off auto top-ups on accounts in observation is a simple but effective hard rule.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Results and SOP Standardization</h2>
<p>After running the full inspection:</p>
<ul>
<li>the five non-compliant creatives were recut into compliant versions and passed review, restoring approval rates;</li>
<li>the appeal for the main account was completed with authorization and data-source documentation, and the restriction was lifted within a week;</li>
<li>the new backup account went live successfully, and the team no longer experienced a blackout whenever the main account went down.</li>
</ul>
<p>More importantly, the process was standardized into weekly routine actions:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Monday: creative pre-screening</strong> — every new creative passes the six-item checklist; nothing ships without a pass.</li>
<li><strong>Wednesday: account health check</strong> — inspect spend anomalies, rejection-reason distribution and account status labels.</li>
<li><strong>Friday: balance and reconciliation</strong> — reconcile spend, top-up records and account balances to catch duplicate charges or missing entries.</li>
<li><strong>Monthly: landing page and authorization review</strong> — clear expired authorizations, dead links and outdated claims.</li>
</ol>
<h2>A Short Comment</h2>
<p>Most teams treat compliance as something to fix after a rejection, and pay for it in lost traffic and creative capacity during the restriction. The real savings from compliance inspection come from treating shifts in rejection-reason distribution as an early signal: when one rejection category rises two weeks in a row, it usually means the team's creative style or landing-page structure is drifting systematically — far earlier than an account ban would reveal it.</p>
<p>For teams running multiple accounts across multiple markets, account tiering plus top-up isolation plus balance alerts is essentially buying operational redundancy with a compliance buffer, at a cost far below a single account freeze.</p>
